  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Cooler than average and mainly dry weather lasts \nthrough at least midweek.\n\nAn omega-blocking type pattern will remain in place across the CONUS \nthrough at least the first half of next week. This will sandwich the \neastern Great Lakes between a reloading upper level trough across \nthe Northeast and a strong high pressure ridge spanning the nation's \nmidsection and the Canadian Prairies. The former will maintain the \nflow of unseasonably cool air (single digit 850mb temps) across the \nregion while the latter provides the necessary subsidence for dry \nweather to prevail. Embedded shortwaves coupled with diurnal \ninstability and modest lapse rates within the eastern trough may \ncause a few showers or a thunderstorm to clip the North Country at \ntimes. Highest chances for this will be Sunday afternoon and \nevening, with relatively lower chances again on Tuesday.\n\nThe coldest timeframe through the next 7 days will be today and \ntonight as highs at the lower elevations only manage the low/mid \n60s, with low temperatures tonight in the low/mid 40s. Upper 30s at \nthe higher elevations tonight with light winds and clear skies could \nlead to some frost and valley fog, though thinking remains on track \nthat any frost would be too patchy and brief to warrant headlines. \nTemperatures then moderate back towards average by Sunday and remain \nseasonable through midweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Trending warmer later in the week, with more \nunsettled weather possible by next weekend.\n\nThe aforementioned omega-block will begin to break down and shift \neastward around midweek. This will allow for the warmer airmass \nunder the ridge to the west to finally advect into the region, \ncausing surface temps Thursday onwards to swing above average for \nearly June. \n\nEarly signals from long range guidance suggest that by next weekend, \nthe omega-blocking pattern will have broken down and shifted \neastward such that the trough on the western side of the ridge will \nfinally begin moving into the Great Lakes. This would bring another \nbout of unsettled weather back to the region, though confidence in \nthe details is low given the forecast range.",
